            <ArticleTitle>Health barriers to walking for exercise in elderly primary care.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>258-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Health status is acknowledged by nursing theory as a factor in the probability of health behavior changes. Yet few studies have addressed the health-related barriers encountered by chronically ill elderly patients in primary care clinics who are trying to increase their physical activity. This study used self- and interviewer-administered instruments to assess potential barriers to physical activity in a sample of 60- to 80-year-old patients entering a walking program. Pain, fatigue, and mobility and sensory impairments were prevalent and could be significant barriers to participation. The authors present specific suggestions for helping patients overcome these barriers.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
